    "title": "Antibiotic Time-Kill Curve",
    "field": "Immunology / Microbiology",
    "plotType": "Log10 viable-count time series across antibiotic concentrations",
    "description": "A time-kill assay tracks the number of viable bacteria (log10 CFU/mL, from plate counts) over time after exposure to an antibiotic at several multiples of its MIC, alongside an untreated growth control. On the log10 axis, killing appears as a downward, roughly straight decline whose steepness increases with drug concentration, while the control climbs and then levels off at stationary phase. A drug is called bactericidal at a concentration if it produces at least a 3-log10 (99.9%) drop from the starting inoculum.",
