    "id": "immuno-zone-of-inhibition-bar",
    "title": "Zone-of-Inhibition Bar Chart",
    "field": "Immunology / Microbiology",
    "plotType": "Bar chart of disk-diffusion inhibition-zone diameters with error bars and a breakpoint line",
    "description": "In a Kirby-Bauer disk-diffusion test, antibiotic-impregnated disks are placed on a lawn of bacteria and the clear ring where growth is suppressed is measured as a zone-of-inhibition diameter. This chart plots that diameter (in mm) for each antibiotic in the panel, with error bars from replicate plates and a dashed reference line at the clinical breakpoint. Bars are colored by whether the isolate is susceptible or resistant relative to that breakpoint.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Antibiotic disk",
        "unit": "category"
      },
      {
        "name": "Zone of inhibition diameter",
        "unit": "mm"
      },
      {
        "name": "Susceptibility breakpoint",
        "unit": "mm"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "A larger zone means the organism is more sensitive to that antibiotic, because growth is suppressed farther from the disk. Comparing each bar against the breakpoint line classifies the isolate as susceptible or resistant for each drug, which is exactly how a lab guides antibiotic choice. The error bars show how reproducible the measurement is; a bar sitting right on the breakpoint with overlapping error is an ambiguous intermediate result that needs confirmation (for example by an MIC method).",
